e for column n-2, we would run out of vertical lines before all columns are covered.\" This argument seems plausible but we need to verify its correctness.\n\nBut before that, we need to verify the solution's reasoning for the upper bound on k. The solution's reasoning is somewhat informal. Let's examine each part.\n\nThe solution's structure:\n\n- Section 1: Preliminaries: defines T_n, counts points, defines sunny lines, column sizes.\n\n- Section 2: Points on a sunny line: analysis of slope p/q, shows that the sum x+y values of integer points on a sunny line form an arithmetic progression with difference |p+q|. Then they claim that a sunny line can contain at most n+1 points of T_n, and more importantly, it can meet each column in at most one point (otherwise vertical). This is correct: a non-vertical line cannot have two points with same x-coordinate, so at most one per column.\n\n- Section 3: Upper bound for number of sunny lines: They argue that each sunny line meets any fixed column in at most one point. Hence for any column, at most k of its points can be covered by sunny lines; the remaining points must be covered by non-sunny lines. Then they discuss non-sunny lines: vertical, horizontal, slope -1. Horizontal and slope -1 lines also meet each column in at most one point, while vertical lines meet a whole column. Therefore, to cover a column that contains more than k points we need at least one vertical line among the non-sunny lines.\n\n- Then they consider the three rightmost columns (sizes given by (2)). They argue: If k ≤ 2 then column x=n-2 (size 3) cannot be covered solely by sunny lines; consequently one of the non-sunny lines must be vertical and must be the line x=n-2.\n\n- Then they look at column x=n-1 (size 2). If we do not use a vertical line x=n-1, both its points must be covered by sunny lines, i.e., each sunny line must contain exactly one of them. The remaining column x=n contains a single point (n,1). A line joining (n,1) with either of the